  "result": "Default `[]` built once, at `def` time, stored in `add.__defaults__`. Every call without `items` gets that same list object. `add(1)` appends 1 to it, `add(2)` appends 2 to the same list, so result is `[1, 2]`.\n\nFix: use `None` sentinel, make new list per call:\n\n```python\ndef add(x, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\n```\n\nNow `add(1)` \u2192 `[1]`, `add(2)` \u2192 `[2]`.\n\nSame bug with `{}`, `set()`, or any mutable default. Immutable defaults (`0`, `\"\"`, `()`, `None`) are safe.",
